msgid "Interactive data visualization using ``Panel``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:26
msgid ""
"Please run the following code examples in a notebook environment otherwise "
"the interactive parts of this tutorial will not work. You can use the button "
"\"Download Jupyter notebook\" at the bottom of this page to download this "
"script as a Jupyter notebook."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:31
msgid ""
"The library `Panel <https://panel.holoviz.org/index.html>`__ can be used to "
"create interactive dashboards by connecting user-defined widgets to plots. "
"``Panel`` can be used as an extension to Jupyter notebook/lab."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:35
msgid "This tutorial is split into three parts:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:37
#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:61
msgid "Make a static map"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:38
#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:95
msgid "Make an interactive map"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:39
#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:142
msgid "Add a grid for Earth relief"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:43
msgid "Import the required packages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:47
msgid ""
"import numpy as np\n"
"import panel as pn\n"
"import pygmt\n"
"from pygmt.params import Axis\n"
"\n"
"pn.extension()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:63
msgid ""
"The `Orthographic projection <https://www.pygmt.org/dev/projections/azim/"
"azim_orthographic.html>`__ can be used to show the Earth as a globe. Land "
"and water masses are filled with colors via the ``land`` and  ``water`` "
"parameters of :meth:`pygmt.Figure.coast`, respectively. Coastlines are added "
"using the ``shorelines`` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:72
msgid ""
"# Create a new instance or object of the pygmt.Figure() class\n"
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    # Orthographic projection (G) with projection center at 0° East and\n"
"    # 15° North and a width of 12 centimeters\n"
"    projection=\"G0/15/12c\",\n"
"    region=\"g\",  # global\n"
"    frame=Axis(grid=30),  # Add gridlines in steps of 30 degrees on top\n"
"    land=\"gray\",  # Color land masses in \"gray\"\n"
"    water=\"lightblue\",  # Color water masses in \"lightblue\"\n"
"    # Add coastlines with a 0.25-point thick pen in \"gray50\"\n"
"    shorelines=\"1/0.25p,gray50\",\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:97
msgid ""
"To generate a rotation of the Earth around the vertical axis, the central "
"longitude of the Orthographic projection is varied iteratively in steps of "
"10 degrees. The library ``Panel`` is used to create an interactive dashboard "
"with a slider (works only in a notebook environment, e.g., Jupyter notebook)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:104
msgid ""
"# Create a slider\n"
"slider_lon = pn.widgets.DiscreteSlider(\n"
"    name=\"Central longitude\",  # Give name for quantity shown at the "
"slider\n"
"    options=list(np.arange(0, 361, 10)),  # Range corresponding to "
"longitude\n"
"    value=0,  # Set start value\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@pn.depends(central_lon=slider_lon)\n"
"def view(central_lon):\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    Define a function for plotting the single slices.\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    # Create a new instance or object of the pygmt.Figure() class\n"
"    fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"    fig.coast(\n"
"        # Vary the central longitude used for the Orthographic projection\n"
"        projection=f\"G{central_lon}/15/12c\",\n"
"        region=\"g\",\n"
"        frame=Axis(grid=30),\n"
"        land=\"gray\",\n"
"        water=\"lightblue\",\n"
"        shorelines=\"1/0.25p,gray50\",\n"
"    )\n"
"    return fig\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"# Make an interactive dashboard\n"
"pn.Column(slider_lon, view)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:144
msgid ""
"Instead of using colors as fill for the land and water masses a grid can be "
"displayed. Here, the Earth relief is shown by color-coding the elevation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/working_with_panel.rst:149
msgid ""
"# Download a grid for Earth relief with a resolution of 10 arc-minutes\n"
"grd_relief = pygmt.datasets.load_earth_relief(resolution=\"10m\")\n"
"\n"
"# Create a slider\n"
"slider_lon = pn.widgets.DiscreteSlider(\n"
"    name=\"Central longitude\",\n"
"    options=list(np.arange(0, 361, 10)),\n"
"    value=0,\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"@pn.depends(central_lon=slider_lon)\n"
"def view(central_lon):\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    Define a function for plotting the single slices.\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    # Create a new instance or object of the pygmt.Figure() class\n"
"    fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"    # Set up a colormap for the elevation in meters\n"
"    pygmt.makecpt(\n"
"        cmap=\"SCM/oleron\",\n"
"        # minimum, maximum, step\n"
"        series=[int(grd_relief.data.min()) - 1, int(grd_relief.data.max()) + "
"1, 100],\n"
"    )\n"
"    # Plot the grid for the elevation\n"
"    fig.grdimage(\n"
"        projection=f\"G{central_lon}/15/12c\",\n"
"        region=\"g\",\n"
"        grid=grd_relief,  # Use grid downloaded above\n"
"        cmap=True,  # Use colormap defined above\n"
"        frame=Axis(grid=30),\n"
"    )\n"
"    # Add a horizontal colorbar for the elevation.\n"
"    fig.colorbar(annot=2000, tick=1000, label=\"Elevation\", unit=\"m\")\n"
"    return fig\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"# Make an interactive dashboard\n"
"pn.Column(slider_lon, view)"
msgstr ""

